reneuro -- provenance of the shipped models
================================================================================

The models in data/ are converted from PyPSA-Eur (https://github.com/PyPSA/pypsa-eur)
networks built at the ELECTRICITY-ONLY endpoint, `base_s_{N}_elec.nc`. The
sector-coupling workflow was not run, which matters for licensing: several
PyPSA-Eur inputs with restrictive terms are reachable only from
`rules/build_sector.smk` and therefore never entered these models.

CODE LICENCE: Apache-2.0 -- BUT THE WORKING PACKAGE IS AGPL-3
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All R sources here are original work under the Apache License 2.0. No 'energyRt'
source is copied; only its exported API is called.

However, 'energyRt' is AGPL-3 and reneuro lists it in Imports. R links imported
namespaces into one process, so a distribution of reneuro TOGETHER WITH
'energyRt' -- the only form in which reneuro is usable -- is a combined work
that must be conveyed under AGPL-3. Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with
AGPL-3, so that combination is lawful.

Do not read "Apache-2.0" as "permissive in practice". What it does buy is that
the conversion code alone -- the netCDF reader, the reimplementation of PyPSA's
apply_line_types(), the Kirchhoff cycle-basis construction, and the
piecewise-linear loss tranches -- is reusable WITHOUT 'energyRt' under
permissive terms.

DATA LICENCE: ODbL-1.0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Required by the share-alike terms of the OpenStreetMap-derived inputs (`osm`,
`osm_boundaries`), which supply the transmission topology. ODbL propagates to
the derived database, so the converted models carry it.

This is independent of the code licence above.

THE NUTS GEOSCALE CARRIES AN ADDITIONAL SOURCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`nuts_gs` is built from PyPSA-Eur's `nuts3_shapes.geojson`, which combines two
sources beyond those listed below:

  Eurostat NUTS 2021 ..... (c) European Union. Reuse is authorised under the
                           Commission's reuse policy (2011/833/EU) provided the
                           source is acknowledged. Boundary data is
                           "administrative boundaries: (c) EuroGeographics".
  OpenStreetMap adm1 ..... ODbL-1.0, for BA, MD, UA and XK, which NUTS does not
                           cover.

The ODbL terms already stated below therefore apply to `nuts_gs` as well, and
the Eurostat attribution is required in addition.

ATTRIBUTION REQUIRED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  OpenStreetMap contributors ......... ODbL-1.0
  European Environment Agency ........ CC-BY-4.0  (Natura 2000 protected areas)
  Copernicus ......................... land cover
  ECMWF / ERA5 ....................... reanalysis weather
  PyPSA-Eur authors .................. workflow and assembled inputs
  European Union / Eurostat .......... NUTS 2021 (nuts_gs only)
  EuroGeographics .................... administrative boundaries (nuts_gs only)

PyPSA-Eur's own per-input inventory is `doc/data_inventory.csv` upstream.

WDPA IS ABSENT, AND THIS IS VERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSUMED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The World Database on Protected Areas (protectedplanet.net, (c) UNEP-WCMC and
IUCN) may not be redistributed "in whole or in part". It is the one input that
could not be carried into a redistributable model, even in derived form.

It is not present. In the build these models came from:

  * `wdpa_available()` in rules/build_electricity.smk treats WDPA as OPTIONAL:
    present -> used; absent -> the exclusion is skipped with a warning.
  * Neither data/wdpa/WDPA.gpkg nor data/wdpa_marine/WDPA_WDOECM_marine.gpkg
    exists in the source clone.
  * Every determine_availability_matrix_MD_UA log records
    "WDPA not available - skipping protected-area exclusion".

CONSEQUENCE, stated because it is a real modelling caveat: WDPA is used in one
place only -- excluding protected areas in Moldova and Ukraine, which Natura
2000 does not cover. With it skipped, land available for wind and solar in MD
and UA is OVERSTATED, and renewable potential with it. Natura 2000 coverage
elsewhere -- including all EU nodes -- is unaffected.

Note that PyPSA-Eur's `natura:` flag gates BOTH datasets. Disabling it to drop
WDPA would also drop Natura 2000, which is CC-BY-4.0 and freely
redistributable, degrading land availability across every node for no licensing
benefit. It is left enabled.

INPUTS WITH UNCLEAR UPSTREAM TERMS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two electricity-demand series are listed as licence "unknown" in PyPSA-Eur's own
inventory:

  opsd_electricity_demand    Open Power System Data
  entsoe_electricity_demand  ENTSO-E

Both feed the demand profiles in these models. The uncertainty is upstream and
unresolved; it is recorded here rather than smoothed over. Anyone with a
stricter redistribution requirement than ours should resolve those terms
directly with the owners before relying on the shipped demand series.

INPUTS DELIBERATELY OUT OF SCOPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Reachable only from the sector-coupling workflow, which was not run:

  lau_regions             Eurostat  -- non-commercial download only
  aquifer_data            BGR       -- no right to redistribute
  bfs_road_vehicle_stock  Swiss FSO -- custom (open by ask)
  bfs_gdp_and_population  Swiss FSO -- custom (open by ask)

REGENERATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
data-raw/ holds the scripts that rebuild every object in data/ from a PyPSA-Eur
clone. They record the upstream commit and config used.
